        <h2 id="environmental-regulatory">Environmental &amp; Regulatory Perspective</h2>
        <p>Environmental regulations now strongly influence propellant selection. LPG and DME are classified as volatile organic compounds (VOCs), while HFCs are regulated for climate impact due to their high GWP. In contrast, nitrogen, carbon dioxide, and compressed air are not subject to VOC limits or GWP phase-down programs.</p>

        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Region / Framework</th>
                <th>Key Focus</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>India (PESO)</td><td>Petroleum and explosives safety for hydrocarbon aerosols</td></tr>
              <tr><td>EU (F-Gas Regulation)</td><td>Restricts high-GWP HFCs</td></tr>
              <tr><td>USA (EPA SNAP)</td><td>Controls medical and industrial propellant use</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Global (Kigali Amendment)</td><td>Phase-down of HFCs under Montreal Protocol</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2 id="selection">How to Select the Right Propellant for Your Product</h2>
        <p>Proper propellant selection begins with formulation chemistry and extends through packaging engineering, regulatory classification, transportation safety, and final market positioning. There is no universal propellant suitable for all applications.</p>

        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Decision Factor</th>
                <th>Consideration</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Product chemistry</td><td>Water-based vs solvent-based formulation</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Spray performance</td><td>Target spray pattern and discharge rate</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Safety classification</td><td>Flammability class of finished product</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Regulatory</td><td>Export country import restrictions and VOC rules</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Filling line</td><td>Pressure design limits of available equipment</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2 id="formulation-problems">Common Formulation Problems Linked to Wrong Propellant Selection</h2>
        <p>Incorrect propellant selection is a major root cause of aerosol product failures during pilot production and commercial scaling. Many physical instabilities arise not from the active formulation but from poor propellant compatibility or pressure mismatch.</p>

        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Issue</th>
                <th>Description</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Can deformation</td><td>Bulging or collapse due to pressure imbalance</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Phase stability</td><td>Separation between propellant and formulation</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Spray performance</td><td>Poor spray quality, spitting, or inconsistent discharge</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Valve compatibility</td><td>Chemical attack on valve seals</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Safety escalation</td><td>Excessive flammability classification escalation</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2 id="future">Future of Aerosol Propellants – Trends &amp; Innovations</h2>
        <p>The global aerosol industry is steadily moving toward propellants with lower climate impact, improved safety, and better recyclability compatibility. While hydrocarbons will remain dominant in cost-sensitive markets, specialty and pharmaceutical aerosols are increasingly shifting to compressed gas and low-GWP alternatives.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Growth of bag-on-valve systems with compressed gases</li>
          <li>Increased use of ultra-low GWP propellants</li>
          <li>Transition away from high-GWP HFCs</li>
          <li>Improved valve and actuator efficiency</li>
        </ul>

